 36CA    COULOMB EXCITATION:XUNDL-5    2023DR01                           202308
 36CA c  Compiled (unevaluated) dataset from 2023Dr01: Phys. Rev.C 107, 034306  
 36CA2c  (2023).                                                                
 36CA c  Compiled by G. G~urdal, Apr 13, 2023                                   
 36CA c  Two experiments, intermediate Coulomb excitation and neutron           
 36CA2c  knockout, were performed to measure the B(E2, 0{++}{-1} |) 2{++}{-1})  
 36CA3c  value of {+36}Ca. For the intermediate-energy Coulomb excitation       
 36CA4c  experiment, 76.9 MeV/nucleon {+36}Ca rare-isotope beam was produced    
 36CA5c  from the fragmentation of a 140MeV/nucleon  {+40}Ca primary beam       
 36CA6c  delivered by the Coupled Cyclotron Facility at the National            
 36CA7c  Superconducting Cyclotron Laboratory (NSCL) impinging upon an 800      
 36CA8c  mg/cm{+2} thick {+9}Be production target and separated using a 300     
 36CA9c  mg/cm{+2} Al wedge degrader in the A1900 fragment separator. 11% pure  
 36CAac  {+36}Ca secondary beam was scattered off a 257 mg/cm{++2} thick Au     
 36CAbc  foil located at the target position of the S800 spectrograph. The      
 36CAcc  deexcited |g-rays were detected using GRETINA surrounded S800          
 36CAdc  spectrograph. The GRETINA consisted a total of 48 HPGe crystals; 32    
 36CAec  of them at 90|' and 16 of them at 58|' with respect to the beam        
 36CAfc  direction. The inelastic excitations of the projectile and target      
 36CAgc  nuclei were quantified with the detection of prompt |g-rays in         
 36CAhc  GRETINA. The scattered projectiles were identified using S800 focal    
 36CAic  plane detection system together with tof measurement. Measured: E|g,   
 36CAjc  tof, cross section and B(E2).                                          
 36CA c  The {Ip/|g} branching {I|b{-p}} was measured using the second          
 36CA2c  experiment. A primary 140 MeV/nucleon {+40}Ca beam delivered by the    
 36CA3c  NSCL impinged on a Be target, removed 3 neutrons to produce a {+37}Ca  
 36CA4c  secondary beam. 75 MeV/nucleon {+37}Ca beam impinged on a 0.5 mm       
 36CA5c  thick Be target and the subsequent knock out of one or more neutron    
 36CA6c  populated the excited states of {+36}Ca. The Cesium iodide             
 36CA7c  scintillator array (CEASER) detected the |g-rays while the S800        
 36CA8c  identified the {+36}Ca residues. The protons were detected and         
 36CA9c  identified in the |DE-E (Si-CsI(Tl)) telescope ring array consisted    
 36CAac  of a 1 mm thick S4 DSSD backed by an annular array of CsI detectors.   
 36CAbc  The smaller angles of {+35}K and {+36}Ca residues were measured by     
 36CAcc  the scintillating fiber array (SFA) and the particle identification    
 36CAdc  and momentum of these fragments were measured using S800               
 36CAec  spectrograph. The excitation spectrum of {+36}Ca was determined using  
 36CAfc  invariant-mass method. Measured: E{-|g}, E{-p}, particle               
 36CAgc  identification, momentum distribution of fragments, |g-residue coin,   
 36CAhc  cross sections and {I|b{-p}}.                                          
 36CA c  Comparison with the shell model calculations.                          
